#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "57034318-5a6e-53f6-a8d3-ae8c61692947" :question/math-academy-id "e-641" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/0bb9e5e7315d4bcd5fc21700fdd2597f6d9b585a109145de53c6f94e9530eabf.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ (shown in blue in the picture above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula $1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={-4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092070 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "8b8e3c66-fc60-57ac-baf7-0661e213d01e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7400" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/e5647c0f19727d1e626e637464cc57444e0bc0f2f8e99a4afd15d3c3631dc3f0.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi,$ shown in blue in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} =-7\\cos(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}+(-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+49{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+1+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4db64b8-39e3-5c3b-bc74-edf065084c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-{e}^{-\\pi /4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354601} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf02204-23e8-5e54-a4dd-0835514589fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354602} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cc63ef-146d-5d35-9c03-aa861095956e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354603} {:answer/id #uuid "7de1217e-7df7-5c94-8bd2-95e6bd28e197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354604} {:answer/id #uuid "64de7071-e0e2-52b9-a70e-48a7c56e596b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2cc63ef-146d-5d35-9c03-aa861095956e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354603} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b447a4-4384-55a0-831a-bdb7fbf86d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeba4fa8-124a-54c9-ba6c-4f189488c740" :question/math-academy-id "q-20070" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/fa1c1c3ae2769f45854de4f0b00fc394fccb40b40704cfd09e6eb11eee3c07af.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=3{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{e}^{2\\theta }+9{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{18{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6064233-ed38-5ef3-8349-1dd3a03c75b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091680} {:answer/id #uuid "db306417-a82a-5664-b0d9-2362a93d761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091681} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1e274f-8eb5-5481-b78c-b5fbba99795e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091682} {:answer/id #uuid "6c96387a-f2f3-5c59-a3a3-92a83acc5088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091683} {:answer/id #uuid "74a7db6a-2d70-5ff8-b64d-297fa28f56d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db306417-a82a-5664-b0d9-2362a93d761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091681} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c56fba-9bc3-51b4-a438-d20c93c6b9e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfaa6223-b113-5398-adeb-e8d8b6d36965" :question/math-academy-id "q-9532" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/085be1e624ae99afee256d2a9591f17d2f33f688364b6e59fdcd7c415206bda7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
